  "slug": "differentiation-morphogenesis-6f3d",
  "summary": "Minimal parameterized and evolved developmental models now test how rules yield reproducible morphogenesis and phenotypic differences.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of compact, parameterized models to connect underlying developmental rules to observable reproducibility and phenotypic differences. An agent-based C. elegans model built from four shared components generates both network and traveling-wave collective phases and reproduces N2 versus DA609 differences. Combining live imaging with a minimal four-parameter stochastic branching model shows that Drosophila neuron classes differ in subdiffusive branch dynamics. In a multi-scale Cellular Potts gene-regulatory model, evolved morphologies indicate that cell differentiation underpins reproducible morphogenesis across simulations. An evolved abstract regulatory model further shows phenocopying in developmental trajectories that is absent from random-sampling null models. The newer element is the use of evolution and null-model comparison to test whether reproducibility and phenocopying arise from the rules themselves, although the items do not share a single mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-06-10"
}
